Literals in Java

A literal is a fixed value written directly in code.

🔹 Integer Literal
int a = 10;      // decimal
int b = 0xA;     // hexadecimal
int c = 012;     // octal
int d = 0b1010;  // binary
012 is octal (base 8), not twelve.
🔹 Floating Literal
double d = 10.5;   // default double
float f = 10.5f;   // must add f
 Without f, it becomes double.
🔹 Character Literal
char ch = 'A';
char ch2 = 65;  // ASCII
🔹 Boolean Literal
boolean flag = true;
✅ 2️⃣ Type Casting (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔹 Widening (Automatic)
Small → Large
int a = 10;
double d = a;   // automatic
No data loss.
🔹 Narrowing (Manual)
Large → Small
double d = 10.5;
int a = (int) d;   // explicit casting
Data loss possible.
✅ 3️⃣ Operators in Java
🔹 Arithmetic Operators
+  -  *  /  %
Example:
int a = 10, b = 3;
System.out.println(a / b);  // 3
System.out.println(a % b);  // 1
⚠️ Integer division removes decimal part.
🔹 Relational Operators
==  !=  >  <  >=  <=

Example:

System.out.println(10 > 5);  // true
🔹 Logical Operators
&&  ||  !
Example:
boolean a = true;
boolean b = false;
System.out.println(a && b);  // false
🔹 Assignment Operators
=  +=  -=  *=  /=  %=
Example:
int x = 5;
x += 3;  // x = x + 3
✅ 4️⃣ Increment & Decrement (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔹 Post-Increment
int a = 5;
System.out.println(a++);  // prints 5
System.out.println(a);    // 6
Use first, then increase.
🔹 Pre-Increment
int a = 5;
System.out.println(++a);  // prints 6
Increase first, then use.
🔥 Common exam question area.
✅ 5️⃣ Operator Precedence (Exam Trick)

Order:
1. ()
2. ++ --
3. * / %
4. + -
5. Relational
6. Logical

Example:

int x = 5 + 3 * 2;
System.out.println(x);  // 11

Because multiplication happens first.
